So he kind of kept it.

252
00:13:09,823 --> 00:13:10,823
Mm...

253
00:13:12,726 --> 00:13:16,293
There might be some calcium
and iron and stuff in there.

254
00:13:18,364 --> 00:13:19,875
Are we gonna take this case?

255
00:13:19,899 --> 00:13:23,634
Well, it's an interesting one.

256
00:13:26,272 --> 00:13:28,150
Seventy-five thousand dollars.

257
00:13:28,174 --> 00:13:29,151
That's what he said.

258
00:13:29,175 --> 00:13:31,020
Take off three zeroes.

259
00:13:31,044 --> 00:13:32,421
Seventy-five dollars?

260
00:13:32,445 --> 00:13:34,045
Maybe.

261
00:13:37,651 --> 00:13:39,161
So, what do you think?

262
00:13:39,185 --> 00:13:44,433
Well, on the surface,
I don't think he did it.

263
00:13:44,457 --> 00:13:46,702
Besides, you have to
feel kind of sorry for a guy

264
00:13:46,726 --> 00:13:49,705
who gets swindled by
his own grandmother. Heh.

265
00:13:59,906 --> 00:14:02,151
<i>Natural ingredients
on their skin.</i>

266
00:14:02,175 --> 00:14:04,886
<i>The very ingredients that
have gone into Fountain of Youth</i>

267
00:14:04,910 --> 00:14:06,888
<i>elixir creams.</i> LAWRENCE:
<i>Now, Mr. Ullman,</i>

268
00:14:06,912 --> 00:14:09,826
<i>just what are the
active ingredients?</i>

269
00:14:09,850 --> 00:14:12,762
<i>I'm afraid that's
a trade secret,</i>

270
00:14:12,786 --> 00:14:17,800
<i>but I will say that they
contain no chemicals</i>

271
00:14:17,824 --> 00:14:23,261
<i>or synthetics and
are made... Are...</i>

272
00:14:25,265 --> 00:14:28,065
<i>Are made from
com... Completely...</i>

273
00:14:29,135 --> 00:14:31,146
You think we can
get a copy of that?

274
00:14:31,170 --> 00:14:33,515
Yeah. Sure thing.

275
00:14:33,539 --> 00:14:35,751
Is everything exactly
the way it was that day?

276
00:14:35,775 --> 00:14:37,086
No, uh,

277
00:14:37,110 --> 00:14:38,988
the police locked
everything up after they left

278
00:14:39,012 --> 00:14:41,390
in Mr. Ullman's dressing room.

279
00:14:41,414 --> 00:14:42,892
And where were you
when it happened?

280
00:14:42,916 --> 00:14:44,694
Up here in the booth.

281
00:14:44,718 --> 00:14:45,895
Who was down there?

282
00:14:45,919 --> 00:14:49,264
Jane Barnes and Don
Ridgely were still here,

283
00:14:49,288 --> 00:14:50,666
and, of course, Stephen Spector.

284
00:14:50,690 --> 00:14:52,935
They'd been at a meeting
just before the boss died.

285
00:14:52,959 --> 00:14:54,236
What was that about?

286
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:56,071
I have no idea.

287
00:14:56,095 --> 00:14:57,361
Hm.

288
00:14:58,364 --> 00:15:00,542
Now, Robert Ullman
owned the studio.

289
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:01,811
I... I... I know that.

290
00:15:01,835 --> 00:15:04,279
And Stephen Spector
was his right-hand man.

291
00:15:04,303 --> 00:15:07,116
And what was his
relationship with, um...?

292
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:08,383
What was her name? Jane Barnes?

293
00:15:08,407 --> 00:15:09,384
Yeah.

294
00:15:09,408 --> 00:15:11,353
And, uh... Don Ridgely.

295
00:15:11,377 --> 00:15:12,421
Yeah.

296
00:15:12,445 --> 00:15:15,191
He owned them too, I
guess you could say.

297
00:15:15,215 --> 00:15:17,326
He created their businesses.

298
00:15:17,350 --> 00:15:20,429
Who brought Robert
Ullman the tea?

299
00:15:20,453 --> 00:15:22,464
He made his own. Hot
plate in the dressing room.

300
00:15:22,488 --> 00:15:23,966
Hmm.

301
00:15:23,990 --> 00:15:25,701
A copy of that tape?

302
00:15:25,725 --> 00:15:26,702
Oh, yeah.

303
00:15:26,726 --> 00:15:28,237
Uh, did...? Did, uh, Mr. Ullman

304
00:15:28,261 --> 00:15:30,139
have an office
here in the building?

305
00:15:30,163 --> 00:15:32,030
Right down the hall.

306
00:15:40,674 --> 00:15:41,751
Oh, I'm sorry.

307
00:15:41,775 --> 00:15:43,653
I didn't know
anybody was in here.

308
00:15:43,677 --> 00:15:46,555
I'm Ben Matlock,
Hal Simpson's lawyer.

309
00:15:46,579 --> 00:15:48,879
I'm Stephen Spector.

310
00:15:50,082 --> 00:15:52,428
Oh.

311
00:15:52,452 --> 00:15:56,365
You're the new head of
Mr. Ullman's operation here.

312
00:15:56,389 --> 00:15:58,367
You mind if I ask you
a couple of questions?

313
00:15:58,391 --> 00:15:59,868
Sure do.

314
00:15:59,892 --> 00:16:02,070
If you wanna look
through this office,

315
00:16:02,094 --> 00:16:03,238
you can get a warrant.

316
00:16:03,262 --> 00:16:05,841
Good day.

317
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,132
He was nice and
genuinely interested.

318
00:16:30,156 --> 00:16:31,733
He didn't tell me
what he did for a living.

319
00:16:31,757 --> 00:16:33,802
Oh, come on.
You're not that naive.

320
00:16:33,826 --> 00:16:34,870
I'm sorry.

321
00:16:34,894 --> 00:16:36,238
That's not good enough.

322
00:16:36,262 --> 00:16:38,307
What could I do? He
kept asking me questions.

323
00:16:38,331 --> 00:16:39,308
He was hustling me.

324
00:16:39,332 --> 00:16:41,176
Hustled you?

325
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:43,679
I'm on the brink of
national success.

326
00:16:43,703 --> 00:16:45,881
The video is selling
all over the country.

327
00:16:45,905 --> 00:16:48,183
And you have to tell some
<i>National Informer</i> reporter

328
00:16:48,207 --> 00:16:50,018
that my weight
machines are defective?

329
00:16:50,042 --> 00:16:51,387
Well, they are.

330
00:16:51,411 --> 00:16:53,021
My name is on those machines,

331
00:16:53,045 --> 00:16:54,189
and I stand behind them.

332
00:16:54,213 --> 00:16:55,358
Well, then you're safe

333
00:16:55,382 --> 00:16:57,159
as long as you
don't sit on them.

334
00:16:57,183 --> 00:16:59,094
You're fired.
Pack up your stuff.

335
00:16:59,118 --> 00:17:01,919
Get out of here!

336
00:17:03,055 --> 00:17:05,167
Hi.

337
00:17:15,101 --> 00:17:17,346
Hi.

338
00:17:17,370 --> 00:17:20,716
I'm Ben Matlock,
Hal Simpson's lawyer.

339
00:17:20,740 --> 00:17:22,317
I figured you might be free.

340
00:17:22,341 --> 00:17:24,086
I just saw your
last visitor leave.

341
00:17:24,110 --> 00:17:26,789
Oh, just a small
personnel problem.

342
00:17:26,813 --> 00:17:27,990
How can I help?

343
00:17:28,014 --> 00:17:29,525
Oh, I understand you had

344
00:17:29,549 --> 00:17:31,460
business dealings
with Robert Ullman.

345
00:17:31,484 --> 00:17:35,263
Oh, more than that. We
were old and good friends.

346
00:17:35,287 --> 00:17:36,832
I got him to stop smoking,

347
00:17:36,856 --> 00:17:40,435
change his diet, give
up all caffeine for good.

348
00:17:45,531 --> 00:17:47,031
Hello?

349
00:17:48,067 --> 00:17:50,212
Yes.

350
00:17:50,236 --> 00:17:51,236
Yes.

351
00:17:55,241 --> 00:17:56,640
I understand.

352
00:17:57,677 --> 00:17:59,176
Okay.

353
00:18:00,312 --> 00:18:03,425
So you and Robert
Ullman were close.

354
00:18:03,449 --> 00:18:05,995
Uh, Mr. Matlock,
I'm really very busy.

355
00:18:06,019 --> 00:18:07,284
If you'll excuse me.

356
00:18:08,621 --> 00:18:11,399
Well, I thought we
having a nice little...

357
00:18:11,423 --> 00:18:14,803
I really must bring this
conversation to an end.

358
00:18:14,827 --> 00:18:16,604
Who was that on the phone?

359
00:18:16,628 --> 00:18:18,473
Goodbye, Mr. Matlock.

360
00:18:20,166 --> 00:18:21,666
Goodbye.

361
00:18:36,816 --> 00:18:38,193
Can I help you with that?

362
00:18:38,217 --> 00:18:39,662
Oh, thank you.

363
00:18:39,686 --> 00:18:41,196
This is quite an operation.

364
00:18:41,220 --> 00:18:42,497
Tell me about it.

365
00:18:42,521 --> 00:18:45,990
Actually, I was kind of
hoping you could tell me.

366
00:18:49,895 --> 00:18:51,373
Oh, yeah.

367
00:18:51,397 --> 00:18:54,042
Yeah, there's a fortune to
be made in today's market.

368
00:18:54,066 --> 00:18:56,778
I love it, love it, love it.

369
00:18:56,802 --> 00:18:58,546
Providing, of course,
you've got a big bundle

370
00:18:58,570 --> 00:19:00,682
to sink into Quick
Opportunity Real Estate.

371
00:19:00,706 --> 00:19:02,351
"Sink" is my middle name.

372
00:19:03,842 --> 00:19:07,589
I'll tell you. Let me just,
uh, suggest a distributorship.

373
00:19:07,613 --> 00:19:08,690
Uh-huh.

374
00:19:08,714 --> 00:19:10,058
If you, uh... If you buy in

375
00:19:10,082 --> 00:19:12,694
on the top rung of the ladder,

376
00:19:12,718 --> 00:19:15,430
you can enjoy the type of
life and benefits that I have.

377
00:19:15,454 --> 00:19:18,066
And if I'm truly successful,

378
00:19:18,090 --> 00:19:19,968
I might even have
furniture in my room.

379
00:19:21,660 --> 00:19:22,904
Sit down, please. Thank you.

380
00:19:22,928 --> 00:19:24,506
Oh, we're in a
transitional stage.

381
00:19:24,530 --> 00:19:25,507
We just moved in.

382
00:19:25,531 --> 00:19:28,610
Mmm. It's quite impressive.

383
00:19:28,634 --> 00:19:30,078
Now, considering
the amount of money

384
00:19:30,102 --> 00:19:31,447
that I'm thinking
about investing

385
00:19:31,471 --> 00:19:33,449
with you in your company,

386
00:19:33,473 --> 00:19:37,820
I feel obligated to ask
about Robert Ullman's death.

387
00:19:37,844 --> 00:19:39,354
What about it?

388
00:19:39,378 --> 00:19:42,624
Well, it places a cloud
over this operation.

389
00:19:42,648 --> 00:19:44,726
Ha-ha-ha. I'm afraid I
don't quite understand.

390
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:46,328
Well, your relationship
with Bob Ullman

391
00:19:46,352 --> 00:19:48,297
went beyond that cable station.

392
00:19:48,321 --> 00:19:51,667
I mean, he had an ownership
position in your business.

393
00:19:51,691 --> 00:19:53,936
You might even say
he was a... A partner.

394
00:19:53,960 --> 00:19:56,405
What makes you
privy to all of this?

395
00:19:56,429 --> 00:19:58,106
Well... Wait, don't answer that.

396
00:19:58,130 --> 00:20:01,943
I think I know.

397
00:20:01,967 --> 00:20:05,981
I don't think you're
interested in real estate at all.

398
00:20:06,005 --> 00:20:07,549
No, I think you have been hired

399
00:20:07,573 --> 00:20:11,153
to investigate Bob
Ullman's death,

400
00:20:11,177 --> 00:20:14,956
and because he and I
had business dealings,

401
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:17,092
then I'm a suspect,
right? Well...

402
00:20:17,116 --> 00:20:18,994
I have to terminate this
conversation right now.

403
00:20:19,018 --> 00:20:20,895
But we making such
excellent progress.

404
00:20:20,919 --> 00:20:22,131
I think you'd better leave.

405
00:20:22,155 --> 00:20:23,499
Are you throwing me out?

406
00:20:23,523 --> 00:20:24,799
Yes.

407
00:20:24,823 --> 00:20:26,568
Whoa!

408
00:20:26,592 --> 00:20:29,993
Well, just as long as
we're upfront about it.

409
00:20:37,603 --> 00:20:39,982
How are you doing,
fellas? What you doing?

410
00:20:40,006 --> 00:20:42,550
You work for Mr. Ridgely?

411
00:20:42,574 --> 00:20:43,551
Could be.

412
00:20:43,575 --> 00:20:45,654
Might give him a message.

413
00:20:45,678 --> 00:20:47,923
Hey, wait a minute.
What are guys...? Whoa!

414
00:20:47,947 --> 00:20:50,425
All right, all right, all
right. All right, all right.

415
00:21:12,004 --> 00:21:13,214
Does this stuff work?

416
00:21:13,238 --> 00:21:15,406
How old do I look?

417
00:21:17,276 --> 00:21:18,353
Forty-five.

418
00:21:18,377 --> 00:21:19,976
I'm 62.

419
00:21:21,481 --> 00:21:23,993
Sixty-two?

420
00:21:24,017 --> 00:21:25,194
And you use this?

421
00:21:25,218 --> 00:21:30,165
Absolutely. I use
it every morning.

422
00:21:30,189 --> 00:21:31,354
How old are you?

423
00:21:32,925 --> 00:21:34,191
Thirty-nine.

424
00:21:37,930 --> 00:21:39,408
You wanna try it?

425
00:21:39,432 --> 00:21:40,409
I might.

426
00:21:40,433 --> 00:21:42,110
Good. I'll get you some.

427
00:21:42,134 --> 00:21:43,545
Thank you.

428
00:21:43,569 --> 00:21:46,181
Would you...? Would you
remind Mr. Spector that I'm here?

429
00:21:46,205 --> 00:21:47,549
Ben Matlock.

430
00:21:47,573 --> 00:21:50,919
Of course, Mr. Matlock.

431
00:21:50,943 --> 00:21:54,678
This is outrageous. How
dare you? We're filled.

432
00:21:56,082 --> 00:22:00,195
I'm sorry, but
Mr. Matlock's been waiting.

433
00:22:00,219 --> 00:22:03,620
Uh, okay. I can't
talk to you now.

434
00:22:06,925 --> 00:22:09,371
You'll see a big difference
if you use it regularly.

435
00:22:09,395 --> 00:22:10,372
Oh.

436
00:22:10,396 --> 00:22:12,240
You might really be 39.

437
00:22:13,265 --> 00:22:14,425
You can go in now. Thank you.

438
00:22:18,204 --> 00:22:19,247
Thirty-nine.

439
00:22:25,143 --> 00:22:27,756
Mr. Matlock, I thought
I told you already

440
00:22:27,780 --> 00:22:29,458
I have nothing
more to say to you.

441
00:22:29,482 --> 00:22:31,626
Uh, I know.

442
00:22:31,650 --> 00:22:33,495
Uh, what I don't understand

443
00:22:33,519 --> 00:22:36,798
is why you told Don
Ridgely and Jane Barnes

444
00:22:36,822 --> 00:22:38,934
they didn't have
anything to say to me.

445
00:22:38,958 --> 00:22:42,371
I don't decide for anyone
else, just for myself.

446
00:22:42,395 --> 00:22:46,207
All right. Let's start with you.
Why won't you talk to me?

447
00:22:46,231 --> 00:22:50,813
Mr. Matlock, I'm taking over
Robert Ullman's business.

448
00:22:50,837 --> 00:22:52,036
Why should I?

449
00:22:55,641 --> 00:22:56,685
Okay.

450
00:22:58,678 --> 00:23:00,855
I guess we'll talk in court.

451
00:23:00,879 --> 00:23:02,791
See you then.

452
00:23:02,815 --> 00:23:03,792
Wrong door.

453
00:23:03,816 --> 00:23:05,849
I know.

454
00:23:19,898 --> 00:23:21,543
Hey!

455
00:23:21,567 --> 00:23:23,066
Hey!

456
00:23:24,803 --> 00:23:26,970
Hey, open the door!

457
00:23:43,588 --> 00:23:44,632
Did you lock me in here?

458
00:23:44,656 --> 00:23:47,090
I don't even know you.

459
00:23:59,204 --> 00:24:01,950
Mr. Matlock, you can't go in.

460
00:24:01,974 --> 00:24:05,587
Mr. Spector, somebody just
locked me in your broom closet.

461
00:24:05,611 --> 00:24:07,355
It was the same person
who was in your office

462
00:24:07,379 --> 00:24:08,990
just before me, and I
wanna know who it was.

463
00:24:09,014 --> 00:24:10,224
There was no one in this office.

464
00:24:10,248 --> 00:24:11,359
I heard him yelling at you,

465
00:24:11,383 --> 00:24:14,062
and I saw the shadow
of his feet under that door.

466
00:24:14,086 --> 00:24:16,531
Ha-ha. I'm afraid
you're imagining things.

467
00:24:16,555 --> 00:24:17,766
Imagine this.

468
00:24:17,790 --> 00:24:19,601
I put you on the stand

469
00:24:19,625 --> 00:24:21,436
and I turn you
every way but loose.

470
00:24:21,460 --> 00:24:24,773
I prove that you had
motive and opportunity

471
00:24:24,797 --> 00:24:26,175
to kill Robert Ullman.

472
00:24:26,199 --> 00:24:29,745
That not only provides my
client with reasonable doubt,

473
00:24:29,769 --> 00:24:31,245
but it hits you where it hurts,

474
00:24:31,269 --> 00:24:33,014
right in the middle
of your rating

475
00:24:33,038 --> 00:24:36,539
at Dun and Bradstreet.

476
00:24:38,543 --> 00:24:40,421
There were a lot of people
with reason to kill Bob.

477
00:24:40,445 --> 00:24:41,723
Who?

478
00:24:41,747 --> 00:24:44,292
Don Ridgely and
another, Jane Barnes.

479
00:24:44,316 --> 00:24:45,694
Why?

480
00:24:45,718 --> 00:24:46,961
Why?

481
00:24:46,985 --> 00:24:49,097
Heh-heh. Because Robert Ullman

482
00:24:49,121 --> 00:24:51,934
was taking them to
the cleaners, that's why.

483
00:24:51,958 --> 00:24:53,902
Taking a piece of
their opportunity.

484
00:24:53,926 --> 00:24:56,003
Soaking them at
the studio for airtime.

485
00:24:56,027 --> 00:25:00,975
But now that he's dead,
they are free of his deal.

486
00:25:00,999 --> 00:25:03,144
You don't plan on
leaving town, do you? No.

487
00:25:03,168 --> 00:25:04,768
'Cause I'm coming back.

488
00:25:22,922 --> 00:25:25,567
What the hell are
you doing in my car?

489
00:25:25,591 --> 00:25:29,671
First of all, technically,
this is no longer your car.

490
00:25:29,695 --> 00:25:32,040
It's been repossessed,
which I know

491
00:25:32,064 --> 00:25:34,810
because I just made last
month's payment on your loan.

492
00:25:34,834 --> 00:25:38,246
Now, if you're wondering what
am I doing in your former car,

493
00:25:38,270 --> 00:25:40,114
I'm looking into
a little secret.

494
00:25:40,138 --> 00:25:44,752
I'm finding out why
this is your former car.

495
00:25:44,776 --> 00:25:48,256
I'm finding out why you can
barely make house payments

496
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:50,559
or furnish your rooms.

497
00:25:50,583 --> 00:25:52,360
Temporary cash flow problem.

498
00:25:52,384 --> 00:25:54,762
It's embarrassing,
but nothing serious.

499
00:25:54,786 --> 00:25:56,465
Very embarrassing for people

500
00:25:56,489 --> 00:26:01,202
whose business is
grossing fortunes.

501
00:26:01,226 --> 00:26:05,640
You see, the truth is
you're going bankrupt

502
00:26:05,664 --> 00:26:09,444
because Robert Ullman was
taking over 50 percent of the gross.

503
00:26:09,468 --> 00:26:13,415
Now, the big secret is
now that Mr. Ullman is dead,

504
00:26:13,439 --> 00:26:14,715
that deal is off.

505
00:26:14,739 --> 00:26:18,253
So the secret is
not only a secret.

506
00:26:18,277 --> 00:26:22,123
It is also a motive for murder.

507
00:26:22,147 --> 00:26:24,414
Okay.

508
00:26:25,751 --> 00:26:29,264
Okay, so I had a motive.

509
00:26:29,288 --> 00:26:30,565
That's all.

510
00:26:31,757 --> 00:26:34,469
Sorry. I don't mean
to disappoint you,

511
00:26:34,493 --> 00:26:38,006
but thanks for
getting my car back.

512
00:26:38,030 --> 00:26:40,108
I didn't say you're
getting the car back.

513
00:26:40,132 --> 00:26:42,177
You're eight payments behind.

514
00:26:42,201 --> 00:26:43,412
See, the payment that I made

515
00:26:43,436 --> 00:26:46,081
was just for the
use of the car today,

516
00:26:46,105 --> 00:26:47,882
