605
00:31:11,570 --> 00:31:12,981
<i>Call in and stay tuned</i>

606
00:31:13,005 --> 00:31:17,007
<i>to see who America
believes killed Robert Ullman.</i>

607
00:31:24,482 --> 00:31:26,261
Ben, can you hear me?

608
00:31:26,285 --> 00:31:27,661
<i>Michelle,</i>

609
00:31:27,685 --> 00:31:29,864
I'm in, uh, Robert
Ullman's dressing room.

610
00:31:29,888 --> 00:31:31,232
Did you see me come in?

611
00:31:31,256 --> 00:31:34,802
No, and if this is where
Ken Pritchard was

612
00:31:34,826 --> 00:31:37,338
prior to the murder, he
didn't have a clear view

613
00:31:37,362 --> 00:31:39,206
of Robert Ullman's
dressing room at all.

614
00:31:39,230 --> 00:31:42,009
<i>Do you still think he
really knows who did it?</i>

615
00:31:43,302 --> 00:31:48,149
Yeah, but if somebody
slipped in and out of here

616
00:31:48,173 --> 00:31:50,552
without being seen from
up there and obviously did,

617
00:31:50,576 --> 00:31:54,189
I really don't know how.

618
00:32:19,304 --> 00:32:21,483
Maybe I do know how.

619
00:32:21,507 --> 00:32:23,184
You see this?

620
00:32:23,208 --> 00:32:24,819
Tea. Yeah?

621
00:32:24,843 --> 00:32:27,756
It's herb tea.

622
00:32:27,780 --> 00:32:31,426
You have that copy of
Robert Ullman's last tape?

623
00:32:31,450 --> 00:32:32,960
Yes. Right here.

624
00:32:32,984 --> 00:32:34,484
Put it on.

625
00:32:43,094 --> 00:32:44,961
Okay.

626
00:32:46,465 --> 00:32:48,142
I'll hit play.

627
00:33:06,118 --> 00:33:08,629
<i>Alpine villages.</i>

628
00:33:08,653 --> 00:33:11,532
<i>I noticed the people there
looked strong and beautiful.</i>

629
00:33:11,556 --> 00:33:15,837
<i>Their skin was healthy
and wrinkle-free.</i>

630
00:33:15,861 --> 00:33:19,173
<i>And then I
discovered two things.</i>

631
00:33:19,197 --> 00:33:23,344
<i>They were all...</i>

632
00:33:23,368 --> 00:33:28,383
<i>But I will say that they
contain no chemicals</i>

633
00:33:28,407 --> 00:33:35,457
<i>or synthetics and
are made... Are...</i>

634
00:33:35,481 --> 00:33:40,817
<i>Are made from com...
Completely... Ingre...</i>

635
00:33:43,788 --> 00:33:46,134
<i>Call Ken Pritchard to the stand.</i>

636
00:33:46,158 --> 00:33:48,636
Mr. Pritchard,

637
00:33:48,660 --> 00:33:51,839
you were present
while Robert Ullman

638
00:33:51,863 --> 00:33:54,775
taped his final cable
television show.

639
00:33:54,799 --> 00:33:55,810
Is that correct?

640
00:33:55,834 --> 00:33:56,978
Yes, of course.

641
00:33:57,002 --> 00:33:59,747
And besides yourself
and the other people

642
00:33:59,771 --> 00:34:00,949
who were always there...

643
00:34:00,973 --> 00:34:03,952
Stephen Spector, the cameramen.

644
00:34:03,976 --> 00:34:06,454
Who else was at
the studio that day?

645
00:34:06,478 --> 00:34:10,392
Don Ridgely, Jane Barnes,
and that gentleman there.

646
00:34:10,416 --> 00:34:12,727
Yes, the defendant, of course.

647
00:34:12,751 --> 00:34:17,064
Uh, now, to the best
of your recollection,

648
00:34:17,088 --> 00:34:20,635
did, uh, Mr. Ridgely
and Miss Barnes

649
00:34:20,659 --> 00:34:23,805
often attend the tapings
of Mr. Ullman's shows?

650
00:34:23,829 --> 00:34:25,039
No, hardly ever.

651
00:34:25,063 --> 00:34:28,042
Would you tell the court
why they were there that day?

652
00:34:28,066 --> 00:34:30,845
For a business
meeting with Mr. Ullman.

653
00:34:30,869 --> 00:34:35,583
Do you know what Mr. Ullman's
business relationship was

654
00:34:35,607 --> 00:34:37,551
with Mr. Ridgely
and Miss Barnes?

655
00:34:37,575 --> 00:34:39,320
Not the exact details.

656
00:34:39,344 --> 00:34:41,623
But as the station manager,

657
00:34:41,647 --> 00:34:44,492
you must have sent out
the bills for their studio time.

658
00:34:44,516 --> 00:34:45,793
Yes.

659
00:34:45,817 --> 00:34:49,164
And how would you
characterize Mr. Ullman's rates?

660
00:34:49,188 --> 00:34:51,032
I'd say they were high.

661
00:34:51,056 --> 00:34:52,199
Aha.

662
00:34:52,223 --> 00:34:55,737
Did, uh...? Did Mr. Ridgely
and Miss Barnes

663
00:34:55,761 --> 00:34:57,772
ever complain about their bills?

664
00:34:57,796 --> 00:34:59,541
Yes. What'd they say?

665
00:34:59,565 --> 00:35:01,275
Well, they felt like
they were being robbed.

666
00:35:01,299 --> 00:35:04,979
They felt they could get cheaper
studio time anywhere else.

667
00:35:05,003 --> 00:35:07,048
Were they angry? Yes.

668
00:35:07,072 --> 00:35:08,816
Furious? Yes.

669
00:35:08,840 --> 00:35:10,618
Why didn't they
go someplace else?

670
00:35:10,642 --> 00:35:13,821
I understand that their
contracts with Mr. Ullman

671
00:35:13,845 --> 00:35:16,791
required them to make
their shows with his facilities.

672
00:35:16,815 --> 00:35:18,226
And that's why they were angry.

673
00:35:18,250 --> 00:35:19,828
Yes, very angry.

674
00:35:19,852 --> 00:35:22,163
So, uh... Ahem.

675
00:35:22,187 --> 00:35:24,365
How long had you
known Robert Ullman?

676
00:35:24,389 --> 00:35:28,269
He hired me as station manager

677
00:35:28,293 --> 00:35:31,272
and chief engineer of WULL-TV

678
00:35:31,296 --> 00:35:33,374
about seven years ago.

679
00:35:33,398 --> 00:35:34,476
I've known him that long.

680
00:35:34,500 --> 00:35:35,643
Was it a good relationship?

681
00:35:35,667 --> 00:35:37,478
Excellent. Were you friends?

682
00:35:37,502 --> 00:35:40,615
No. We were business
associates. That's all.

683
00:35:40,639 --> 00:35:43,251
But in those seven years,

684
00:35:43,275 --> 00:35:46,654
you must've noticed, you
know, several idiosyncrasies,

685
00:35:46,678 --> 00:35:47,888
little things about him.

686
00:35:47,912 --> 00:35:48,923
Yes, I suppose.

687
00:35:48,947 --> 00:35:50,424
Yeah, yeah. Did he drink coffee?

688
00:35:50,448 --> 00:35:53,094
Never. Uh, he was
off caffeine completely.

689
00:35:53,118 --> 00:35:54,261
Oh. Tea?

690
00:35:54,285 --> 00:35:55,563
Only herbal tea.

691
00:35:55,587 --> 00:35:56,830
Ah.

692
00:35:56,854 --> 00:35:58,466
Now, Mr. Pritchard,

693
00:35:58,490 --> 00:36:04,004
uh, I show you the
People's Exhibit 7.

694
00:36:04,028 --> 00:36:08,442
Now, this is the
teapot that contained

695
00:36:08,466 --> 00:36:11,512
a lethal dose of the
deadly poison monocaine.

696
00:36:11,536 --> 00:36:12,980
Do you recognize it?

697
00:36:13,004 --> 00:36:15,984
It appears to be the teapot
from Mr. Ullman's dressing room.

698
00:36:16,008 --> 00:36:18,419
It is the teapot. Mm.

699
00:36:18,443 --> 00:36:20,142
And these...

700
00:36:22,413 --> 00:36:28,796
are the regular English
breakfast tea bags

701
00:36:28,820 --> 00:36:30,198
that were found in the pot.

702
00:36:30,222 --> 00:36:31,687
Anything unusual about them?

703
00:36:34,026 --> 00:36:37,071
Uh, they're... They're not
herbal tea bags, are they?

704
00:36:37,095 --> 00:36:40,841
And Mr. Ullman would never
drink regular tea, correct?

705
00:36:40,865 --> 00:36:42,744
No. He would not. Objection.

706
00:36:42,768 --> 00:36:44,946
Calls for speculation.
No foundation.

707
00:36:44,970 --> 00:36:47,515
Your Honor,
Mr. Pritchard has just said

708
00:36:47,539 --> 00:36:49,884
that Mr. Ullman
didn't drink regular tea.

709
00:36:49,908 --> 00:36:51,518
Your Honor, there's a difference

710
00:36:51,542 --> 00:36:52,954
between didn't
drink and wouldn't.

711
00:36:52,978 --> 00:36:53,955
Oh, come on!

712
00:36:53,979 --> 00:36:56,179
Sustained.

713
00:37:10,562 --> 00:37:13,507
Mr. Pritchard, would
you please tell the court

714
00:37:13,531 --> 00:37:17,411
exactly where you were

715
00:37:17,435 --> 00:37:18,880
when Mr. Ullman died.

716
00:37:18,904 --> 00:37:21,783
I was in the director's booth.

717
00:37:21,807 --> 00:37:24,918
So you had a clear view
of the whole set at all times.

718
00:37:24,942 --> 00:37:25,919
Is that right?

719
00:37:25,943 --> 00:37:27,688
Yes, I did. Okay.

720
00:37:27,712 --> 00:37:31,725
Uh, with the court's
permission and, uh, your help,

721
00:37:31,749 --> 00:37:35,396
I'd like to reenact
the final moments

722
00:37:35,420 --> 00:37:36,997
before Mr. Ullman collapsed.

723
00:37:37,021 --> 00:37:38,633
Proceed, Mr. Matlock.

724
00:37:38,657 --> 00:37:39,968
Okay.

725
00:37:39,992 --> 00:37:44,706
Ahem. Now, you're
seated as Mr. Ullman was

726
00:37:44,730 --> 00:37:48,142
when he was answering
questions in that sham interview.

727
00:37:48,166 --> 00:37:51,813
Well, it was like...
In a chair. Yes.

728
00:37:51,837 --> 00:37:57,218
And while he was talking
about Alpine villages

729
00:37:57,242 --> 00:37:59,887
and wrinkle-free skin,
what was he doing?

730
00:37:59,911 --> 00:38:01,555
In accordance with the script,

731
00:38:01,579 --> 00:38:04,958
